<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Thymbra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/546-bce">546 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Thymbra was fought in 546 BCE in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a>. Cyrus then captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> coast under Persian rule. The victory was a key step in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-thymbra.jpg" alt="Battle of Thymbra" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Walter Hutchinson 1877 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Defeat_of_Croesus_546_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Cyrus conquered the Kingdom of Media in 550 BCE, which brought him into conflict with the neighbouring Lydian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>. After the drawn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pteria">Battle of Pteria</a>, Cyrus pursued Croesus into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and hoped to catch the Lydian king unprepared. Croesus had partly disbanded his army, but he armed his reserves and marched out to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> before his allies could arrive, and they never did. The two armies met on the plain north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>.</p><p>The size of the armies rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>. He gives Cyrus 196,000 men, including Persian infantry, peltasts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, Arabians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and Medians, together with 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> cavalry, 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> that each held 20 men. He gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> 420,000, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a>, Cappadocians, peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and 120,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. His figures, even if untrue, are considered within the realm of possibility, though less than half of the men may have taken part in the fighting. The Lydian army was about twice the size of the Persian one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Cyrus drew up his troops in a square formation with the flanks held back and covered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, cavalry and infantry. Baggage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> formed a barrier around his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and their smell disturbed the Lydian horses and broke up the cavalry charge while the archers shot into the Lydian ranks. As Cyrus had expected, the Lydian wings wheeled inward to envelop the square, and gaps opened at the hinges of the turning wings.</p><p>Fire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and the mobile towers inside the square added to the disorder. Cyrus then ordered his flank units forward, and they struck the disorganised Lydian wings. The Lydian cavalry lost many men and retreated, and most of the infantry surrendered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> escaped with a small part of the infantry to his capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives no numbers, but his account of the course and outcome of the battle agrees with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> were driven within the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, which fell after a siege of 14 days, reportedly because the defenders had left unguarded a part of the wall they thought too steep to attack. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> was captured, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, with the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a>, became part of the Persian empire. The Egyptian soldiers who had fought for Croesus surrendered and joined Cyrus's army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says Cyrus treated Croesus with respect, while the Babylonian Nabonidus Chronicle appears to report that Cyrus killed the Lydian king, though which king it means is unclear. The conquest brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> into the contact that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a> of Cyrus's successors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Thymbra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He then defeated Croesus of Lydia, traditionally at the <b>Battle of Thymbra</b>, and captured Sardis around 547–546 BCE, bringing the Ionian Greek cities of the Aegean coast under Persian suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
